course: politics
lesson: 41
title: Olympiad: fair votes, close counts and who decides ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This olympiad lesson turns the course into puzzles: count the votes, check the rule, follow the chain of who decides, and watch for one word in the question that changes everything.
explain: "Most votes" and "more than half" are not the same: with 30 voters, 12 votes can win a race of three, yet 18 people chose someone else, and more than half would mean at least 16.
explain: The rule can change the winner: count once, or knock out the last one and pass their papers to each voter's next choice, like numbering boxes in Australia, or win group by group like the US states, and the same papers can crown a different winner.
explain: Who-decides puzzles are chains: a bill needs both parts of Parliament or Congress and then the King's assent or the president's signature, while a broken swing in the park goes to the council, not the Prime Minister.
explain: People exaggerate and play things down about votes too: "literally everyone voted for me" may mean 40 kids out of 200, and "I lost by a teeny bit" may mean 3 votes to 27, so always count the real numbers.
fact: Winning with the most votes is not the same as winning more than half: more than half of 30 is at least 16, and more than half of 25 is at least 13.
fact: In Australia voters number the candidates in order; if nobody has more than half, the last candidate is knocked out and those papers go to each voter's next choice, again and again until someone has more than half.
fact: When votes are won group by group, as in the US Electoral College, a candidate can win the most groups with fewer votes in total; this has happened in some US presidential elections, and people disagree whether it is fair.
fact: While a count is going on, a candidate is only sure to win when the other cannot catch up even if every vote still to count goes to them.
fact: A UK bill that starts in the House of Commons must pass the Commons and the House of Lords and then get Royal Assent from the King; a US bill must pass the House and the Senate and then be signed by the president.
fact: If a US president vetoes a bill, Congress can still make it law if two out of every three members in both the House and the Senate vote for it again.
fact: The elected Commons usually gets its way over the Lords in the end, and by custom the King always gives Royal Assent; no monarch has refused for over 300 years.
fact: Local councils look after things like parks, bins, libraries and small roads; the national government decides big national things like the army.
fact: A UK Parliament petition gets a government reply at 10,000 signatures and is considered for a debate at 100,000, but a debate does not mean the law changes.
fact: A poll's margin of error is its wobble: if one party is 2 points ahead and the wobble is 3, the race is too close to call.
fact: In a show of hands people can see each other's votes, so some vote to please friends; a secret ballot lets everyone vote as they really think.
fact: Mayors, governors and premiers are chosen in elections or by elected bodies, but the King is not elected; he stays neutral and does not vote.
fact: Words in this lesson: "wicked" in New England means very; "mardy" in the North and Midlands of England means grumpy; "heaps" in Australia means very or lots; "fixin' to" in the US South means about to; "delulu" means hoping for something that won't happen.
exercise: Class 5B votes for a trip: zoo 12, museum 10, beach 8. Which statement is true? | The zoo won the most votes, but more people chose something else | The zoo won more than half of the votes, so the whole class agreed | The beach won, because the smallest group gets the final say | Nobody won, because no trip got exactly half of all the votes
exercise: Same trip vote (zoo 12, museum 10, beach 8), but counted the Australian way. Beach is knocked out, and all 8 beach voters put museum second. Who wins? | Museum, 18 to 12 | Zoo, 12 to 10 | Zoo, because the beach voters go home and stop counting | A tie, 15 to 15
exercise: 25 children vote for class rep. What is the smallest number of votes that is more than half? | 13 | 12 | 12 and a half | 15
exercise: Three candidates share 20 votes. Amir has 9 and Bella has 6. Could Chloe have won? | No, she has only 5, fewer than both | Yes, the last one counted always wins | Yes, if she also got 9 of them | Maybe, we need to know her age
exercise: 30 votes; 20 counted so far: Ava 11, Ben 9. How many of the last 10 must Ava get to be sure of winning? | 5 | 4 | 6 | 10
exercise: Three tables of 5 vote table by table, like US states: table 1 gives Ana 5 and Ben 0; tables 2 and 3 each give Ben 3 and Ana 2. What is the result? | Ben wins two tables, though Ana has 9 votes to his 6 | Ana wins two tables and has more votes, 9 to 6 | Ben wins two tables and has more votes, 9 to 6 | Ana wins two tables, though Ben has 9 votes to her 6
exercise: A bill passes the US House of Representatives, but the Senate votes no. What happens? | It does not become law, because both parts must say yes | It becomes law, because the House has more members | The president can sign it into law anyway, because the House agreed | The Supreme Court votes in place of the Senate
exercise: A bill starts in the House of Commons. Put its journey in order. | Commons votes, Lords check it, King gives Royal Assent | King gives Royal Assent, Commons votes, Lords check it | Lords check it, King gives Royal Assent, Commons votes | Commons votes, King gives Royal Assent, Lords check it
exercise: A school council of 9 copies the US veto rule: the head can say no, but two out of every three members can overrule her. How many votes overrule her? | 6 | 5 | 7 | 9
exercise: A park swing is broken, a motorway needs new lanes, and the army needs new boots. Who usually decides about the swing? | The local council | The national government | The army itself | The King, by Royal Assent
exercise: A petition for longer playtime has 64,000 signatures. What has it already earned? | A government reply, but not yet a debate | A debate in Parliament and a brand-new law | A new law, starting tomorrow morning | Nothing at all until it reaches a million
exercise: A petition reaches 100,000 and MPs debate it. Ruby says the law has now changed. Is she right? | No, a debate puts the idea to MPs but changes no law | Yes, every petition that is debated becomes a law | Yes, but only once the King has signed the petition in person at the palace | No, because MPs never read petitions at all
exercise: A poll says Party A 45, Party B 43, with a wobble (margin of error) of 3. What is the fair headline? | Too close to call | Party A is sure to win | Party B is secretly ahead | Party A wins by a landslide
exercise: 28 pupils vote by show of hands: 20 for football, 8 for art. Next day, a secret vote of the same 28: 14 to 14. What best explains it? | Some had voted to please friends when everyone could see | Some children were away on the second day, so the numbers dropped | Secret votes are always counted wrongly | In a show of hands each hand counts twice
exercise: Odd one out: a mayor, a governor, a premier, the King. Which is odd, and why? | The King, the only one not chosen by an election | The mayor, the only one with no power at all | The governor, the only one who is never elected | The premier, the only one who lives in Europe
test: Your mate brags, "Literally the whole school voted for me, bruv!" The board says 40 of 200. What really happened? | He got 40 votes; "the whole school" was an exaggeration | He got all 200 votes, just as he said | He got 160, everyone who did not vote against him on the day | He got no votes, since 40 is not the whole school
test: Cousin Kai texts, "lost class rep by a teeny bit lol, like 3 to 27." How close was it really? | Not close: the winner got nine times as many votes | Very close, as close as a vote can get | A tie, so they must hold the vote again | Close, he only needed 3 more votes to win
test: Jake from Texas: "y'all, how'd the president win with fewer votes across the whole country??" What is the best answer? | The race is won state by state, so the national total can lose | The votes from Texas were all thrown away | It must be a mistake, since more votes in the country always win the race | Votes from red states count double everywhere
test: Grandad in Boston: "Wicked close count. Ava 11, Ben 9, ten to go. Ava's got it in the bag!" ("wicked" means very.) Is he right? | No, Ben still wins if he gets 7 or more of the last 10 votes | Yes, whoever leads after 20 votes always wins | Yes, because Ben can only tie at the very best | No, because Ben has already won the count
test: Little sis, a bit mardy: "The Prime Minister broke our swing and he won't fix it!" ("mardy" means grumpy.) Who should the family write to? | The council | The Prime Minister, since he runs every park | The army, since it fixes everything | The King, since he owns all the swings
test: In Brisbane, Mia says, "Heaps easy, I numbered the boxes 1 to 3." Her number 1 came last and was knocked out. Was her vote wasted? | No, it moved to her number 2 | Yes, her paper went straight in the bin | Yes, she must vote again next week | No, it counted twice for her number 1
test: "My uncle says the King can block any law he hates, no cap." What is true? | By custom he always gives Royal Assent | Yes, the King blocks a law most weeks | Yes, but only laws about his castles | No, because the King writes every law himself
test: Delulu Dan: "I got 9 of the 20 votes, so that's more than half!" ("delulu" means hoping for what won't happen.) What's wrong? | More than half of 20 is at least 11, so 9 falls short | 9 really is more than half of 20 | More than half of 20 is exactly 10 | Nothing; anything close to 10 counts
test: A kid in Georgia: "Fixin' to party, the House passed our bill, it's law now!" ("fixin' to" means about to.) What did he forget? | The Senate must pass it and the president sign it | Nothing; the House makes laws on its own | The King must sign it in London | The Supreme Court must write it first
test: Dad at the telly: "Poll's got us 2 points ahead, it's in the bag, the other lot are toast!" The wobble is 3. Best reply? | Too close to call, Dad: 2 is less than the wobble | Right, any lead in a poll means a win | 2 points means a landslide, Dad | Polls are never wrong, so it's done
test: Gran: "Your council's got a bit of a budget problem, love." The council is shutting two libraries and a pool. What does Gran really mean? | Money is very tight | It is a tiny problem nobody will notice | The council has too much money to spend | The libraries are only shut for a holiday
test: The head vetoes the 9-member council's plan; 5 vote to overrule her under the two-out-of-three rule. Rory: "5 is more than half, we win!" Is he right? | No, they needed 6 | Yes, more than half is always enough to overrule | Yes, because 5 is two out of every three of 9 | No, because nothing can ever overrule a head
exam: It is given that thirty electors cast votes for three candidates, who receive twelve, ten and eight votes respectively. Determine the minimum number of votes requisite for more than half. | Sixteen | Fifteen | Twelve | Eleven
exam: Whereas a candidate leads by fourteen votes to ten with eight ballots yet to be counted, identify the least number of the remaining ballots the leader must receive to be assured of victory. | Three | Two | Four | Eight
exam: Select the sequence consistent with the passage of a bill originating in the House of Commons. | Commons, Lords, Royal Assent | Lords, Royal Assent, Commons | Royal Assent, Commons, Lords | Commons, Royal Assent, Lords
exam: Identify the circumstance in which a candidate may win the greater number of groups while obtaining fewer votes in aggregate. | When each group gives its whole result to the candidate who wins within it | When every vote is counted together across the whole country | When the voters number the candidates in order of preference | When the vote is held by a show of hands in one room
